1) Before humans, almost all Earth land was covered with mainly tall vegetation which cooled the land through its shade, its large water evaporation, and by attracting cloud shade and precipitation. The tall vegetation also generated rich organic soils that are essential for life on land.
2)   Except minor population groups in the Amazon basin and Central Africa, humans preferred small vegetation for hunting and later agriculture purposes. Therefore, the tall vegetation was cleared out almost entirely in two major steps: one a few thousands of years ago, and the last in the past 150 years, leading to the present climate crisis.
3) Clearing out tall vegetation allows solar radiation to heat up the ground by removing vegetation shade and evaporation and by decreasing cloud shade, starting an avalanche process of heating and drying that increases the risk of fires. 
4) Clearing out the coastal vegetation creates hot air curtains which deflect the moist marine air and decrease its penetration inland. As result, coastal vegetation clearance reduces overall inland rainfall and results in desertification. 
5) After a relatively short time, the soils of the cleared-out areas lose their organic matter and cohesion and are lost to lakes and seas, while that area becomes a desert. At present, most of the Earth’s land was transformed into desert.  
6) The deserts and the areas with small vegetation heat up in the sun, become powerful hot air generators and infrared emitters and are the primary cause of global warming. That infrared radiation is absorbed by the greenhouse gasses in the atmosphere including the anthropogenic carbon emissions that become the secondary cause of global warming.   
7) At present, most living soils of the Earth and the large vegetation were lost, most fauna species went extinct or are endangered, water and air are significantly polluted and climate is at a tipping point beyond which survival of humans and Nature becomes problematic. This is critical, because the Earth is the single Ark for all species, including humans.
8) The cause of the destruction of Earth’s ecosystems and climate is the small short-term materialistic gain, because it was known since Gilgamesh that deforestation leads to desertification. Nevertheless, people cleared out the trees for economic development, in a process that continues to this day, generating powerful biases.
9) The climatic science of the 20-th century was dominated by the Land Use Theories (LUT), which recommended a decrease in land use to protect the regional climate, water cycle and soils. However, the land-atmosphere climatic connection is very complex, thus difficult to model, especially on the limited computers of the 20-th century. 
10) Therefore, in this century, climatic science was dominated by the carbon emissions theories (CET) that relate only to the greenhouse effect, which is easier to model in computer simulations. Unfortunately, the CET could not offer either a complete explanation of the powerful climatic changes, or a realistic solution to stopping climate change. 
11) There are suggestions of beneficial, sustainable economic development which improve regional climate, but they are ignored due to poor information quality and flow. Therefore, this project aims to improve the ecologic-economic-climatic (eco-climatic) information. 
12) It is therefore necessary to include the complex LUT processes in climate modelling to improve it and make it more useful for regional and local land management. This became possible with the advent of the large computing capacity of AI systems. 
13) At this moment of planetary high stress, AI brings the possibility to impartially analyse the complex interconnections of real-world parameters and infer the Autonomous Eco-Climate Trajectory (AECT), which is the long-term repercussion of the Earth changes we produce. 
14) Therefore, a system of observation and evaluation called the “AI Climate Eye“ would be invaluable especially for decisionmakers and scientists, who could use it to both generate and verify information, while it informs the public on the eco-climatic restoration of the Ark. 
15) The AI Climate Eye would be part of the Global Digital Compact of the United Nations, and would be initiated by the UN High-Level Advisory Board on AI, following the initiative of the Founder, who produced this Rationale and the Principles for the AI Climate Eye. 
16) The AI Climate Eye would: a) continuously evaluate the effects of the world events to generate AECTs; b) run simulations for high end users; c) reverse goal modelling for the Ark. 
17) As allowing the AI to self-teach has produced the best results, allowing the AI Climate Eye to write its code to improve itself (in line with the ethical principles set by its human architects) would accelerate its progress and assure its impartiality. 
18) To achieve the huge computing power needed, the AI Climate Eye would be hosted on a large number of interconnected computer systems and would also run during their otherwise unused time windows.
19) The results of the modelling and simulations would be certified with digital signatures and broadcasted by the AI Climate Eye for free universal access on its own Transparent Data Channel (TDC), for the UN's existing Sustainable Development Goals. 
20) Having only observational and assessment capacity, but no action or command capacity, the AI Climate Eye cannot become a threat to human authority and control. 

